workedExamples.question_text:
A report claims: “Pool visits increased every month.” Check the claim against the graph and write a supported alternative.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=0.01cm]
\path[use as bounding box] (-1.8,-90) rectangle (4.9,450);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(4.8,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,440);
\foreach \v in {0,100,200,300,400} {\draw[black] (-0.08,\v)--(0,\v); \path (-0.12,\v) node[left,text=black,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \x/\month/\count in {1/Jan/240,2/Feb/360,3/Mar/300,4/Apr/400} {\draw[black] (\x-0.3,0) rectangle (\x+0.3,\count); \path (\x,0) node[below,text=black,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {\month};}
\path (-1.5,220) node[rotate=90,text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {Visits};
\path (2.5,-65) node[text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {Month};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
A company’s press release is headed "**Downloads soar quarter after quarter**", and attaches the figures below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.72]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,11.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(13.5,0);
\foreach \y in {10,20,30} {\draw (-0.2,\y/3)--(0,\y/3); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y/3) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.8,0) rectangle (2.3,6.0);
\draw (3.1,0) rectangle (4.6,8.0);
\draw (5.4,0) rectangle (6.9,7.33);
\draw (7.7,0) rectangle (9.2,9.0);
\draw (10.0,0) rectangle (11.5,8.67);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.55,0) {Q1};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.85,0) {Q2};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6.15,0) {Q3};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (8.45,0) {Q4};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (10.75,0) {Q5};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,5) {Downloads (1000s)};
\node[below] at (6.15,-1.0) {Quarter};
\node at (6.15,11.1) {Downloads per quarter};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Does the graph support the headline?
Justify your answer with the figures.

development.question_text:
A local paper reports "**Club membership has grown every year since $2019$**", and prints the club’s figures below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(9.4,0);
\foreach \y in {200,400,600,800,1000} {\draw (-0.15,\y/200)--(0,\y/200); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,\y/200) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,0) {$0$};
\foreach \x/\lbl in {1/2019,2.4/2020,3.8/2021,5.2/2022,6.6/2023,8.0/2024} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0) {\lbl};}
\draw[thick] (1,3.1)--(2.4,3.9)--(3.8,3.5)--(5.2,4.3)--(6.6,4.75)--(8.0,4.55);
\foreach \p in {(1,3.1),(2.4,3.9),(3.8,3.5),(5.2,4.3),(6.6,4.75),(8.0,4.55)} {\fill \p circle (2.5pt);}
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.5,2.5) {Members};
\node[below] at (4.5,-1.0) {Year};
\node at (4.5,6.1) {Club membership};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which part of the claim does the graph contradict, and what claim would the graph support?

mastery.question_text:
A drink company’s advertisement is headed "**Zest — the lowest sugar of any brand**" and prints the graph below.
In the small print, a serve of Zest is $200$ mL and a serve of every other brand shown is $350$ mL.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.72]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,9.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(11.2,0);
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10,12} {\draw (-0.2,{\y*0.7})--(0,{\y*0.7}); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,{\y*0.7}) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.8,0) rectangle (2.3,4.2);
\draw (3.1,0) rectangle (4.6,6.3);
\draw (5.4,0) rectangle (6.9,5.6);
\draw (7.7,0) rectangle (9.2,7.7);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.55,0) {Zest};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.85,0) {Nimbu};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6.15,0) {Orla};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (8.45,0) {Pax};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.2,4.2) {Sugar (g)};
\node[below] at (5.0,-1.0) {Brand};
\node at (5.0,9.5) {Sugar per serve};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
State what the graph does support, and explain what a reader must ask before accepting the headline.
